<RevealLayout
  title="The 2x2x2 Rubik's Cube"
  description="Efficient representations and traversal of the Rubik's Cube state space"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The Rubik's Cube has an absurd number of possible configurations. Far
        too many to explore, even with a modern computer. But what about its
        little brother, the 2 by 2? Is its universe small enough to explore
        completely?
      </p>
      <p>
        And not just explore it. Can we compute the minimum number of moves
        needed to solve every single cube? Spoiler: yes, we can. And we'll do it
        in Python, directly in the browser. But getting there is not trivial.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <CubeCompare client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section id="intro">
    <LogoAnimation
      client:load
      width={2080}
      height={600}
      pixelScale={8}
      duration={2700}
      loop={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Before doing any math or writing any code, we need to be precise about
        what we are measuring. What does it mean to say that a cube is, for
        example, 7 moves away from solved? Well, that depends on what counts as
        a move.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the cubing community, each face is named relative to the current
        orientation of the cube. So the right face does not mean a fixed color.
        It simply means whatever face is on the right from our point of view.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, the orange face might currently be on the right. But if we
        rotate the whole cube, that same orange face might become the front face
        instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using this notation, an R move means a clockwise turn of the right face.
        Its inverse, R prime, means turning that same face counter-clockwise.
        And we could also decide that a 180 degree turn counts as a basic move.
        In that case, we call it R2. So the minimum number of moves depends on
        which moves we take as basic.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Once we know which moves are allowed, the problem becomes much easier to
        think about. We can represent all cube configurations as a graph. Each
        configuration is a node. And there is an edge between two nodes if one
        allowed move transforms one state into the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        To make this concrete, imagine that we only allow one move: R. Starting
        from the solved cube, applying R once gives us a new state. Applying it
        again gives another. Two more turns bring us back to solved.
      </p>
      <p>
        So if we only allow R, the entire reachable universe has just four
        states.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now notice something important. If we only allow R, one of those states
        is 3 moves away from solved. But if we also allow R prime, that same
        state is only 1 move away. So the minimum number of moves depends on
        which moves we allow.
    </p>
    <p>
        If only quarter turns count as single moves, this
        is called the quarter-turn metric. If half turns such as R2 also count
        as single moves, this is called the half-turn metric.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no need to define R3, because that is just the same as R prime.
        So for each face, we really have three meaningful moves: clockwise,
        counter-clockwise, and half turn.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikStateGraph
      client:only="react"
      initialIndex={0}
      initialDepth={4}
      initialMoves={["R", "R'", "R2"]}
      overflow={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now the problem is well defined. We have a graph. We have a move set. But a computer still needs one more thing.
It needs a way to represent each cube state.
In other words, we need an encoding.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <EncodingShowcase client:only="react" width={1080} height={600} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        There are many ways to do this, but a very natural first idea is to work
        with the stickers. We choose a fixed order, and represent the cube as a
        string where each character tells us the colour of one sticker. Every
        cube state becomes just a different string. That gives us something a
        computer can work with.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      showStateEditor={true}
      cameraRelativeEncoding={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="sticker-perm">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Once the cube is encoded as a string, the next question is how to apply
        moves to it. Each move simply rearranges some of the stickers.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, in the solved cube, an R move sends this yellow sticker
        from the front face to the up face. In our encoding, that sticker is at
        index 9, so after an R move it ends up at index 1.
      </p>
      <p>
        And of course, a move does not affect just one sticker. It moves several
        of them at the same time. It does not matter what the current cube looks
        like. The move always rearranges the same positions in the same way.
      </p>
      <p>
        So in the end, every move is just a fixed reordering of sticker
        positions. In mathematics, that is called a permutation.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <StickerPermutation client:only="react" visibleStickers={24} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        To implement this in Python we just need to translate all those arrows
        into numbers. For example, the R move can be represented as a list of
        the following indices. A 0 in the first position means that the sticker
        that was at index 0 stays there. A 9 in the second position means that
        the sticker that was at index 9 moves to index 1, and so on.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also need a function that takes a cube state and a move, and produces
        the new cube state.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`MOVES = {
    "R": [0, 9, 2, 11, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 21, 10, 23, 14, 12, 15, 13, 3, 17, 1, 19, 20, 18, 22, 16],
    # other moves...
}

# STEP-1
def apply_move(state, move):
    return "".join(state[i] for i in move)`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's simulate an R turn starting from the solved state. Looks like the
        right value.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <PyodideWorkerRunner
      client:only="react"
      initialCode={stickerPermutationCode}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now, how do we explore the cube's state space? We can start from the
        solved state, and apply all possible moves to find all states that are
        one move away. Then we can apply all moves again to those new states to
        find all states that are two moves away, and so on. This is a
        breadth-first search, and it will allow us to find the minimum number of
        moves needed to solve any given state, because we are exploring states
        in order of their distance from solved.
      </p>
      <p>
        A deep-first search would not work, because we might find a solution
        that is not the shortest one. For example, a DFS search might find that
        reaching this state takes 2 moves, if you start exploring the R edges
        first. But if you explore the R2 edges first, you would find a shorter
        solution that takes only 1 move.
      </p>
      <p>
        It helps to visualize the search as a wave that expands from the solved
        state, reaching states that are 1 move away, then 2 moves away, and so
        on. The set of states you can reach with n moves is also called a
        frontier, because it is the frontier of the search at that point.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikStateGraph
      client:only="react"
      initialIndex={0}
      initialDepth={4}
      initialMoves={["R", "R'", "R2"]}
      overflow={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="wip">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        With this representation, we can implement a breadth-first search to
        explore the cube's state space. We start from the solved state, and
        apply all possible moves to find all states that are one move away.
      </p>
      <p>
        First we create a visited dictionary to keep track of which states we
        have already seen and how far they are from solved. This is helpful for
        counting how many steps a state is from solved, but can also be used to
        reconstruct a solution. We will talk about this later.
      </p>
      <p>
        We iterate each frontier. At the begining of the iteration we create a
        set that would contain all the states of the next iteration.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then, for each state in current frontier, we apply all possible moves to
        it and get the next state. We check if it is already in visited, and if
        not, we add it to both the visited and to the next frontier.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the end of the depth look we update the frontier. At the end of the
        search we just return the visited array with the depth for each state.
        Using that array we can count how many states there are per depth.
      </p>
      <p>
        Another common way to implement BFS is using a queue. I opted for a
        frontier set instead, which is more efficient for our purposes: we limit
        the number of states we have in the queue to unique states. It's also
        easier to keep track of the depth. Using a set is perfectly valid here
        because we don't really care about the order in which we explore states
        as long as they are at the same depth.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def bfs(start, max_depth):
    # STEP-1
    visited = {start: 0}
    frontier = {start}

    # STEP-2
    for depth in range(1, max_depth + 1):
        next_frontier = set()

        # STEP-3
        for state in frontier:
            for move in MOVES:
                nxt = apply_move(state, move)
                # STEP-4
                if nxt not in visited:
                    visited[nxt] = depth
                    next_frontier.add(nxt)

        # STEP-5
        frontier = next_frontier

    # STEP-6
    return visited`}
    />
  </section>

