  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        There is a fundamental problem in our previous implementation that is
        easier to fix than you think. First of all think about how we numbered
        our stickers, the numbers are not fixed to a specific position on the
        cube, but they are relative to the cube's orientation. For example, if
        we turn the cube, now that the upper face is in front, the sticker
        number 0 is in the upper face. This string is different from the
        previous one, so we are indeed looking at a different state, at least
        that's how the computer sees it. But for us, these two states are
        exactly the same, because they are just different orientations of the
        same cube. Always when working with puzzles and combinatorial objects we
        need to be careful about rotations and symmetries, because they can make
        our state space much larger than it needs to be.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      showStateEditor={true}
      cameraRelativeEncoding={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        We need that somehow, the ids of the sticker to rotate with us, so that
        the string does not change when we rotate the cube. How can we achieve
        that? Well, we can just leave one corner fixed, and only move the other
        7. It doesn't necessary mean to have untouch, but to have it always in
        the same position. Let's say we want to keep fixed this corner, top left
        of the front face. We are free to do any movement we wanto, but after
        encoding this into a state, we need to put it back in the front upper
        left position. In that way, any of the 24 rotations of a given cube will
        be collapsed into one.
      </p>
      <p>
        This also bring us to this curious case, were a right move takes us to
        the same position as a left move. A right move leaves our reference
        piece untouch, but a left move requieres us to do a turn of the cube to
        place it back to its front top left position. The thing is that this
        position is exactly the same as an R turn.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RubikCubeViz
      client:only="react"
      showStateEditor={true}
      cameraRelativeEncoding={false}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        So the solution is pretty simple, we don't need to change the encoding,
        we don't need to deal with rotations, we only need to change the moves
        we do. We need to select 3 of them and it will be enough to leave one
        corner untouched. For example, we can choose R, B and D. With just these
        three moves we can generate all states of the cube ignoring rotations
        and symetries. As we have less moves, it's also faster, and, as we can
        see it runs in around 100s using only 300MB of memory, which is a huge
        improvement from the previous versions.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <PyodideWorkerRunner client:only="react" initialCode={naiveCode} />
  </section>
